            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest major news is that **NASA’s Perseverance rover has found evidence that ancient rocks on the rim of Jezero Crater were built up by repeated asteroid impacts**. NASA/JPL reported on **July 15, 2026** that Perseverance examined a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick layered rock sequence called the **“Broom Point member,”** likely more than **3.9 billion years old**—among the oldest terrain ever studied by a Mars rover. The findings suggest this area preserves a record from a violent early period in solar-system history, before Jezero Crater itself formed【6:2†source】.\n\nNASA’s Perseverance news feed also highlights new views and maps of **Broom Point**, including a sweeping Mastcam-Z view and an orbital map of the rover’s route to the site, both posted July 15, 2026【6:3†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the equivalent of a **full marathon on Mars—26.2 miles / 42.195 km**. It reached that mark on its **1,890th Martian day**, doing so in about **five years and four months**, much faster than Opportunity took to cover the same distance. A Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter HiRISE image captured the rover and its tracks from orbit just before the milestone【6:0†source】.",
                "annotations": [
